# Independent canonical HTML output-budget review

No blocking finding within fixed4de46baf5f22f8dcb5e99d0312d9b61e36a44d6b. This is a read-only code/host-boundary review; root owns production and a separate reviewer owns the authored16 delta. Changed paths, exact Git source bytes and the674 dependency inputs are fixed by snapshot.json/dependencies.json. Only independent probes were appended to the isolated canonical tests module. No production, source document, original log or limit default was edited.

The actual production delta adds one optional typed html_output_limits field with the existing eight-u64 OperationLimits alias and calls the already implemented generate_with_output_budget using a fresh Budget from that field. The complete same budget is passed into existing PageSet resolution, render and serialization; no catch-and-retry, replacement budget or Markdown allowance fallback exists. The parse/lower operations still use unchanged PhaseLimits::default, and source admission/owner/route checks remain in the existing implementation. The registry's existing strict JSON duplicate/property/path validation remains active. A null, partial object, unknown field or inappropriate numeric type is not interpreted as default/unlimited. This is an additive development-host JSON setting, not a new core/wire schema or portable proof.

Independent native tests used a real Doc Article with Japanese Ruby, escaped HTML text and explicit Break. The original production path was restored for the baseline default run; the fixed path ran four independent tests. Final before-exact and after-exact runs restore all five changed code/document files to the corresponding Git commit before each build. The original preliminary before/after controls restored the two production files only, leaving the later unused documentation in place; they are preserved as controls, not described as complete old-source snapshots. Exact runs produce the same results. Commands retain process deadlines, executable/probe hashes and raw stdout/stderr. No independent Linux/WASI run is claimed for this filesystem host slice.

Verified results:

- Old omitted setting, new omitted setting and explicit default setting produce byte-identical HTML, CSS and manifest. The default Article/source/profile values and parse/lower usage remain identical.
- A distinct finite value for every output field is recorded exactly. HTML content identity, file hashes/bytes and each page's parse/lower limits, zero initial usage, actual usage and profile/source provenance remain unchanged. Output execution identity and the derived phase identity change. Their definitions were independently rebuilt with Python SHA256, the exact domain bytes, fixed eight-field u64 big-endian order and zero initial records; both match actual receipts.
- Work0, Allocation0, Nodes0, Depth0, SourceBytes0 and OutputBytes0 each return the corresponding stop with no output directory. These are six tested resource stops, not a claim of eight resource-stop or injected-cancellation coverage. The public registry operation starts a fresh budget; it does not expose a caller cancellation token. Inherited helper cancellation/consumed-budget semantics were read but not newly certified by these public tests.
- Eleven malformed JSON-shaped values and two raw duplicate/overflow forms fail; null, empty/partial objects, arrays/strings/bools, negative/fractional work, null work, unknown fields, duplicate work and u64 overflow are included. A late invalid source and a late HTML route/file-parent conflict also fail before filesystem output. Existing output is rejected.
- Markdown Work0 does not stop HTML with its own valid allowance. HTML Work0 does not stop a separate new-profile Markdown call. Adding HTML configuration changes the raw registry and hence the new Markdown header/context, while its body is identical. This matches the final sentence added to spec16 and is not misreported as metadata byte independence.

The selected output Budget begins before source loading but does not redefine the host's bounded file capture/compilation or each parse/lower operation as output work. A zero output limit may be observed after those separate finite operations, and there is no claim that it prevents every earlier computation. Completion is still emitted only after complete generation. Existing OS write failures may leave an incomplete new directory; this change neither retries such failures nor claims transactional filesystem rollback.

The H five-page100M WorkLimit log and command list are copied unmodified under motivation. They show an observed resolve/render stop at Work99,999,989. That command list does not bind all source/head/executable hashes by itself; this review therefore treats it as the original motivation, not an independently replayed same-input benchmark. Success of a future explicitly budgeted five-page generation, canonical cutover, browser/AX/navigation, deployment, human acceptance and the authored-spec meaning review remain separate scopes.

Reproduction: freeze.py reconstructs the exact dependency set. run.py before <new-label> restores all changed base files and runs the default probe; run.py after <new-label> restores all changed fixed files and runs the four tests. Use fresh destinations and preserve the saved evidence. check.py validates the final exact-run records, SHA identities, source hash and isolated production/probe overlay. The strict manifest allowlist excludes workspace, build cache and binaries; executable hashes remain in command records.
